    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Hail damage shows up as bruises and missing granules on asphalt shingles, dents and cracked coatings on metal, fractures on tile, punctures and crushed insulation on flat roofs, and fresh splits on wood shake. On every roof type, the strongest evidence is a random pattern of impacts concentrated on the slopes that faced the storm, backed by dents in the gutters, vents and other soft metals around the house.</p>
<p>Start from the ground and let a roofer, public adjuster or the insurer's adjuster go on the roof. Tile and steep roofs in particular are easy to damage, and dangerous, to walk.</p>

<h2>Asphalt and architectural shingles</h2>
<p>Most Texas homes have asphalt shingles, either three-tab or the thicker laminated "architectural" type. Hail damages them in a way that's easy to miss from the driveway.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bruises.</strong> A hailstone can crush the asphalt and fracture the fiberglass mat underneath without breaking the surface. Press gently with a thumb: a bruise feels soft, like the skin of a ripe apple, compared with the shingle around it.</li>
<li><strong>Dark, roughly circular spots</strong> where granules were knocked off, exposing black asphalt. Fresh hits look darker and sharper than the surrounding surface.</li>
<li><strong>Fractures and torn edges,</strong> especially at the exposed lower edge (the "tab") and on ridge cap shingles, which take hail head-on.</li>
<li><strong>Granules in the gutters and at downspout outlets</strong> right after the storm, in larger amounts than usual.</li>
</ul>
<p>Architectural shingles have more surface texture and color variation, so bruises are harder to see. Low-angle morning or late-afternoon light helps. Ridge caps on these roofs are often a separate, thinner product and frequently show damage first.</p>
<p>Damage that is <em>not</em> hail, and that adjusters will point to: blistering (small raised bubbles that pop and leave pits, a manufacturing or ventilation issue), thermal cracking in a grid pattern on old shingles, mechanical scuffs from foot traffic, and nail pops. These tend to follow lines or appear evenly on every slope, while hail is random and directional.</p>

<h2>Granule loss vs. normal wear</h2>
<p>Insurers often call granule loss "wear and tear." Sometimes they're right. The difference is in the pattern and timing.</p>
<div class="compare">
<div><h3>Likely hail</h3><ul><li>Granule loss in distinct circular spots, not uniform thinning</li><li>Exposed asphalt looks dark and fresh</li><li>Concentrated on storm-facing slopes</li><li>Heavy granules in gutters right after a known storm date</li><li>Matching dents in nearby soft metals</li></ul></div>
<div><h3>Likely wear</h3><ul><li>Even thinning across whole slopes, worst on the south and west sides that get the most sun</li><li>Loss along drip lines or where water concentrates</li><li>Exposed areas weathered and faded</li><li>Loose granules shed by a new roof in its first months</li><li>No soft-metal damage nearby</li></ul></div>
</div>
<p>A roof can have both. Normal aging doesn't mean hail didn't happen; it means the hail damage has to be counted and photographed separately from the background wear.</p>

<h2>Metal roofs: standing seam and R-panel</h2>
<p>Metal roofs are common on newer Texas homes, rural properties, barns and commercial buildings. They rarely leak from hail, but they dent, and that's where coverage arguments start.</p>
<h3>Standing seam</h3>
<p>Standing seam panels have concealed clips and raised seams that lock together. Look for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Dents across the flat of the panel, easiest to see in raking light or from a drone</li>
<li>Dents on the seams themselves, which can deform the lock and let the seam open</li>
<li>Cracked or crazed paint at the center of dents, sometimes only visible up close</li>
<li>Damaged trim, ridge cap and flashing</li>
</ul>
<h3>R-panel and other exposed-fastener roofs</h3>
<p>R-panel and similar ribbed panels are screwed through the face with rubber-washered fasteners. In addition to dents, check:</p>
<ul>
<li>Fastener heads hit hard enough to crack or displace the washer, which can let water in at the screw</li>
<li>Crushed or flattened ribs</li>
<li>Coating loss that exposes bare steel and leads to rust</li>
</ul>
<p>Many Texas policies carry a cosmetic damage exclusion for metal roofs. Under one, the claim depends on showing that the dents affect function, not just appearance. That fight is covered in <a href="/post/cosmetic-vs-functional-hail-damage/">cosmetic vs. functional hail damage</a>.</p>

<h2>Clay and concrete tile</h2>
<p>Tile roofs are heavy and long-lived, but individual tiles break. Clay and concrete fail differently.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Clay tile</strong> is brittle. Hail causes cracks, often radiating from an impact point, and broken corners or edges. Small fractures may not leak right away but let water reach the underlayment.</li>
<li><strong>Concrete tile</strong> is tougher and more likely to show surface spalling, chipped edges, and impact marks where the color coating has popped off, with cracks from larger hail.</li>
</ul>
<p>The complication with tile is that walking on it breaks it. An inspection itself can crack tiles, and an adjuster may attribute fractures to foot traffic. Before any inspection, have photos taken from a drone or ladder so you have a record of the roof's condition. Look at whether cracks start from a visible impact mark (more consistent with hail) or run across the tile where it rests on the one below (more consistent with being stepped on).</p>
<p>Replacing broken tiles also raises matching questions, since older tile is often discontinued. See <a href="/post/matching-laws-for-roof-replacement-in-texas/">matching and roof replacement in Texas</a>.</p>

<h2>Flat and low-slope roofs</h2>
<p>Flat roofs on homes, additions, apartment buildings and commercial property can't be judged from the ground at all. Common membranes in Texas include TPO, PVC, modified bitumen and built-up roofs, sometimes with a coating.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Punctures and fractures in the membrane,</strong> which may be small and hard to spot without close inspection.</li>
<li><strong>Bruising on modified bitumen,</strong> where the granulated cap sheet loses granules and the mat cracks underneath.</li>
<li><strong>Crushed insulation below the membrane.</strong> The membrane can look intact while the insulation board under it is fractured. Inspectors check this with core cuts, which should be properly patched.</li>
<li><strong>Damage at seams, flashings, drains and rooftop units,</strong> where the membrane is stiffest.</li>
<li><strong>Cracked coatings</strong> on coated roofs, which can let water reach the membrane below.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ponding water on a low-slope roof speeds up leaks through hail fractures, so commercial owners should get flat roofs checked soon after a storm. Our <a href="/commercial-claims/">commercial claims</a> section covers building claims in more detail.</p>

<h2>Wood shake and shingles</h2>
<p>Wood roofs are less common in Texas now, but they're still on older homes. Hail splits wood along the grain.</p>
<ul>
<li>Fresh splits show light-colored, unweathered wood inside the crack, with sharp edges</li>
<li>Look for an impact mark or dent at or near the split, rather than a split running the full length of the shake</li>
<li>Old splits from drying and age are gray inside, with rounded, weathered edges</li>
</ul>
<p>Color inside the split is the most useful clue. Freshly exposed wood weathers within months, so photograph wood roof damage soon after the storm.</p>

<h2>Soft metals: the evidence that proves the storm</h2>
<p>Soft metals are the thin aluminum, copper and painted steel parts around the house: gutters, downspouts, roof vents, turtle vents, pipe jack collars, flashing, window wraps, A/C condenser fins and housings, and metal fences or mailboxes. They dent easily and don't heal, so they show where hail fell, how big it was, and which way the wind drove it.</p>
<p>Adjusters check soft metals early in every inspection. If the gutters and vents show no dents, the carrier will have a hard time believing the shingles were damaged by hail. If they're full of dents on the north and west sides only, that pattern supports damage on the roof slopes that face the same way.</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>Photograph soft-metal dents with a coin or ruler for scale</li>
<li>Rub chalk lightly across a dented gutter or vent cap; the dents show up as clean circles in the chalk</li>
<li>Note which sides of the house have dents and which don't</li>
<li>Look for spatter marks: fresh hail knocks dirt and oxidation off painted and weathered surfaces, leaving bright circular marks that fade within weeks to months, which helps date the storm</li>
<li>Photograph the A/C condenser fins; flattened fins are easy to show and often missed in estimates</li>
</ul>
<p>Soft-metal damage is also a claim item in its own right. Dented gutters, vents and fins all belong on the estimate.</p>

